1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,227
Right now, a complete stranger is drowning in debt, so that the money in your account can exist.

2
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:19,858
That's not a metaphor. If every debt on earth were paid off tonight, your savings would vanish with them...

3
00:00:19,858 --> 00:00:34,594
...Because every dollar you've ever earned was born the same way: not printed, not saved, borrowed into existence.

4
00:00:34,594 --> 00:00:39,181
Give me five minutes, and you'll see the trick with your own eyes.

5
00:00:39,181 --> 00:00:45,828
Why prices never stop rising, why your savings never feel like enough...

6
00:00:45,828 --> 00:00:50,974
...and why this machine needs you in debt, forever.

7
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:55,585
No jargon, just mechanisms.

8
00:00:55,585 --> 00:01:12,240
Walk into a bank and borrow twenty thousand dollars. Most people think the bank takes twenty thousand from someone's savings and hands it to you.

9
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:22,165
That is not what happens. Nobody's account goes down. Not one.

10
00:01:22,165 --> 00:01:31,674
The banker types 2-0-0-0-0 into a screen, and brand new money appears.

11
00:01:31,674 --> 00:01:38,147
Money that did not exist one second earlier.

12
00:01:38,147 --> 00:01:43,751
And if that sounds like a conspiracy theory, here's the fun part.

13
00:01:43,751 --> 00:01:56,449
In 2014, the Bank of England, the world's oldest central bank, published a paper admitting it in plain English:

14
00:01:56,449 --> 00:02:11,167
'When a bank lends, it creates the deposit.' Around 97% of all money in the economy was made exactly this way.

15
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:16,231
Not minted. Typed.

16
00:02:16,231 --> 00:02:26,918
But here's the part they left out of the headline. The bank created your twenty thousand, but you owe back twenty-three.

17
00:02:26,918 --> 00:02:31,207
So where do the extra three thousand come from?

18
00:02:31,207 --> 00:02:34,132
The bank never created them. Nobody did.

19
00:02:34,132 --> 00:02:43,451
The only place that money can exist is inside someone else's loan.

20
00:02:43,451 --> 00:02:55,080
Think about what that means. System-wide, the debt is always bigger than the money. Always.

21
00:02:55,080 --> 00:03:06,154
It's musical chairs with your livelihood. Millions of people scrambling for dollars that don't all exist...

22
00:03:06,154 --> 00:03:13,370
...and the music only keeps playing if new borrowers keep joining the game.

23
00:03:13,370 --> 00:03:26,716
More loans. More debt. Every single year. Not because we're greedy, because the machine stalls the moment we stop.

24
00:03:26,716 --> 00:03:36,384
So who pays for all this new money? You do. Just never as a bill with your name on it.

25
00:03:36,384 --> 00:03:44,910
Every fresh dollar typed into existence quietly shrinks the ones already in your pocket.

26
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:54,427
That's the inflation you feel at the grocery store. The tax you never voted for.

27
00:03:54,427 --> 00:04:12,674
And watch where the new money lands first: Banks, funds, asset owners. They buy before prices rise. Your salary shows up last, after everything already costs more.

28
00:04:12,674 --> 00:04:30,265
And when the machine finally choked in 2008, did it apologize? It sent the bill. 700 billion dollars, to the public that never got a vote.

29
00:04:30,265 --> 00:04:33,629
You weren't bailed out. You were billed.

30
00:04:33,629 --> 00:04:49,881
Now, what if I told you there's a rule set, 1400 years old, engineered to make this exact trick impossible?

